---
/**
 * HeroStack — the homepage hero. A centred type hero with an ambient rig
 * behind it, and NO GRAPHIC.
 *
 * ── WHAT WAS REMOVED, AND ON WHOSE INSTRUCTION ──────────────────────────────
 *
 * Owner, 2026-08-04, reviewing the rebuilt homepage at :8095: "in hero section
 * remove image of Qualify / 2 gaps found before you bid / Win / 82 fit score /
 * Get paid / 4 commitments live".
 *
 * That was V6, "isometric strata": three SVG planes stacked in depth, each with
 * a stage label and one line of product copy beside it, joined by a rail with a
 * light falling down it. Everything that existed to draw it is gone rather than
 * hidden — the STRATA data, the plane geometry constants, the <ol>, ~490 lines
 * of .stratum* / .plane* / .hp-* CSS, the strata-fall keyframes, the seven-selector
 * transition list and the hover block, and the phone breakpoint that unstacked
 * the three columns. Nothing is left behind at opacity 0 or display none.
 *
 * THE THREE FIGURES WENT WITH IT. "2 gaps found before you bid", "82 fit score"
 * and "4 commitments live" were the only product figures in the hero, and this
 * file's header used to carry a paragraph justifying them as fit and rubric
 * coverage rather than as win probability. That paragraph described content
 * that is no longer on the page, so it is deleted rather than left to be read
 * as a live claim. The rule it invoked has not changed and is not this file's
 * to hold: specs/architecture/CODING-STANDARDS.md and specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md
 * still record it, and nothing on this site may state a win rate.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE STAYED ON 2026-08-04, and the owner was explicit about it. "One
 * engine, the whole contract", "Qualify. Win. Get paid.", the standfirst and
 * both buttons were untouched by that pass, as was the closing gate note.
 *
 * ── 2026-08-07: THE OWNER REOPENED THE HEADLINE, AND WHY ────────────────────
 *
 * The 2026-08-04 decision was a decision about COMPOSITION, taken while cutting
 * a graphic. What it did not weigh is that two of the three sentences made
 * claims this site cannot support, and that is what reopened it.
 *
 * "GET PAID." NAMED A PRODUCT THAT DOES NOT EXIST. Nothing on this site covers
 * payment or invoicing. The five stages in HowItRuns end at Evidence; the five
 * screens in ProductScreens end at After award; the nav and the footer offer no
 * payment surface. The eyebrow above it, "One engine, the whole contract",
 * carried the same promise one line earlier. A hero is the one place a reader
 * takes a list of verbs as a list of features, and the third verb had no
 * referent anywhere on the site.
 *
 * "WIN." CONTRADICTED THE PAGE UNDER IT. This page states three times, in the
 * product itself and not only in its marketing, that it does not forecast
 * award: "Relevance is a sorting aid. It is not a forecast of anything.",
 * "Stated as context, never as a probability of award.", and the in-product
 * line quoted in ProductScreens, "This is FIT context. It is not a probability
 * of award, and CrowMark does not produce one." An h1 asserting the outcome the
 * body spends five sections refusing to assert is not a bold headline; it is
 * the page disagreeing with itself, and it invites a reader to price the
 * refusal discipline as marketing rather than as architecture. The rule at line
 * 27 — nothing on this site may state a win rate — was never breached, because
 * no rate was stated. The defect is subtler and is about consistency of stance.
 *
 * WHAT REPLACED THEM, AND THE CONSTRAINT EACH VERB IS UNDER. "Qualify. Draft.
 * Prove it." Every verb now names a stage that is on the page directly below
 * it: Qualify is stage 02, Draft is stage 03, and Prove is stage 05, Evidence.
 * The triad the owner approved is kept as a FORM; only the unsupportable half
 * of its content moved. Refuse, stage 04, is deliberately not in the h1: it is
 * the differentiator, it is the one stage whose job is to remove work, and it
 * is carried by the gate note at the foot of this section and by ProofGate
 * immediately after, where it can be shown rather than asserted.
 *
 * THE EYEBROW NOW CARRIES THE CATEGORY, WHICH IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE FIX.
 * "One engine, the whole contract" described a scope the product does not have
 * and named no category, so a first-time reader met the hero without learning
 * what CrowAgent is. The <title> has said "Bid software: supplier, buyer,
 * public, private" since A-112 and the nav says "Bid and tender software", so
 * the words existed everywhere except where the eye lands first. The eyebrow is
 * now drawn from those two rather than newly invented, and it keeps the both-
 * sides scope A-112 settled: supplier AND buyer, with the market left neutral.
 *
 * ── HOW THE REMAINING CONTENT IS LAID OUT, AND WHY IT IS STILL CENTRED ──────
 *
 * MEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The graphic was never a grid
 * column. It was a SIBLING <ol> under `.hero__inner`, and `.hero__inner` has
 * been a centred single-column stack since the owner's 2026-08-02 decision. So
 * there is no collapsed track to re-lay and no half of a two-up composition
 * left stranded: removing the list removes a block from the bottom of a column
 * and changes nothing about the four elements above it.
 *
 * IT STAYS CENTRED, and the argument is that the alternative is worse. Left
 * alignment would only earn its place if something occupied the right of the
 * row, and now nothing does; a left-aligned hero with 1,064px of measure and
 * 600px of empty air to its right is the composition this site spent a week
 * removing from the legacy pages. Centred, the eyebrow, the h1, the standfirst,
 * the button pair and the gate note all share one axis at 720.
 *
 * THE MEASURE WAS CHECKED AT 1440 AND IS UNAFFECTED. `.hero__lede` caps at 62ch
 * — 813.6px measured, the same cap `.section__standfirst` uses — inside a
 * 1,064px content box, so the standfirst was already narrower than the column
 * and the graphic below it never constrained anything. The h1 sets its own
 * width from its glyphs and renders on one line at 1440. No line length moved.
 *
 * ── THE AMBIENT RIG HAD TO MOVE, AND THAT IS THE ONE REAL CONSEQUENCE ───────
 *
 * The four orbs below were read off the deployed site as ABSOLUTE PIXEL OFFSETS
 * FROM THE TOP OF THE HERO, for a hero that measured 975px tall at 1440. With
 * the graphic gone it measures roughly 508px, and orbs 3 and 4 were pinned at
 * top 520 and top 654 — both of them now entirely below the section's own
 * `overflow: hidden`. Left alone, this change would have silently deleted half
 * the lighting rig and left a hero lit only from the top.
 *
 * So the two LOWER orbs are anchored to the hero's floor instead of its ceiling.
 * Their size, colour, opacity and horizontal position are untouched; only the
 * axis they hang from changed. The offsets are derived from the measurement
 * rather than picked: at the old 975px height orb 3 ended 92px below the floor
 * and orb 4 ended 169px below it, so `bottom: -92px` and `bottom: -169px`
 * reproduce exactly the crop each one had. The two UPPER orbs are unchanged,
 * because the top of the hero has not moved.
 *
 * ── NO JAVASCRIPT, AND NOW NO ANIMATION AT ALL IN THIS COMPONENT ────────────
 *
 * There is none. Every element here is visible and correct from the first
 * paint. The travelling rail light was the last moving part in this file and it
 * belonged to the graphic; with the graphic gone, the only motion the hero
 * carries is the shared ambient drift declared in styles/tokens.css, which is
 * decorative, is inside an aria-hidden subtree, and does not exist under
 * prefers-reduced-motion. Not one word on this page is animated.
 *
 * ── THE PALETTE RULE, AND WHAT IS LEFT OF IT HERE ───────────────────────────
 *
 * The long note this header used to carry was about the three planes and how
 * their hue ramp had to read as light rather than as taxonomy. There are no
 * planes. What survives is the part that governs what is still on screen: the
 * ambient fields are LIGHT and take the full chromatic range, "Prove it." takes
 * --grad-hero-text, and the one MARKER in this component is the teal dot before
 * the gate note, which is the verified role doing the job it does everywhere
 * else. Violet and teal appear nowhere near a control, a hover or a focus ring.
 */
import Button from '../ui/Button.astro';
---

{/*
  `data-spot` is the cursor light's hook (src/scripts/motion.ts). It is written
  as "off" rather than as a bare attribute so the element is selectable before
  any pointer has moved and the stylesheet has one value to test rather than a
  presence check. With no JavaScript it never becomes "on" and the overlay is
  never painted.
*/}
<section class="hero" data-spot="off" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  {/*
    AMBIENT — ONE HUE, TWO LIGHTS, and it was four lights in four hues until
    2026-08-05. A-103, A-111 and A-113 are one cut and this is the hero's share
    of it. The owner's rule, approved that day, is the whole of the argument:

      "A gradient may change LUMINANCE anywhere. It may change HUE once per
       section, on the one element that section is about, and NEVER under text,
       NEVER on a control, NEVER on a state."

    So exactly one field here carries a hue. It is `--1`, the violet source high
    left, because violet is the only hue on this site that names nothing: teal
    is VERIFIED and orchid is REFUSED, and both were being spent as haze on
    every section of the homepage at once, which is A-113 in one sentence.

    THE COMPOSITION IS THE REASON THE SECOND LIGHT SURVIVES RATHER THAN A
    RELUCTANCE TO CUT. The hero's focal point is the teal phrase in the title;
    the field that used to sit behind it was cyan, so the one saturated thing a
    reader is meant to look at was competing with a 605px light of nearly its
    own hue. `--2` is now achromatic: it holds the high-right pole of the
    aurora, so the diagonal survives, and the only chroma left in the frame is
    the phrase itself.

    `--3` (orchid, low centre) and `--4` (teal, low left) are DELETED. They hung
    from the hero floor and were the two fields tinting the ground below it,
    which is the half of A-103 that was still open.

    The vignette is NOT in here. It DARKENS, so it cannot be screen-blended
    with the fields, and it is painted by ::after over the top at normal blend.
    It is a radial in the PAGE colour and never in black.
  */}
  {/*
    `data-field="on"` opts these two into the shared ambient layer declared in
    styles/tokens.css: the always-on --m-drift, and the one-beat m-arrive
    entrance. "on" rather than a bare attribute because the entrance fires at
    LOAD here. Every other section waits for its data-lit to flip; the hero is
    above the fold at every viewport, so it has nothing to wait for.
  */}
  {/*
    THE TWO OVERLAYS GO INSIDE THE WASH, NOT BESIDE IT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
    SAFETY ARGUMENT FOR BOTH. The wash is z-index 0 under a z-index 1 content
    layer, so anything mounted in here is behind every word in the hero by
    construction rather than by a value somebody has to keep correct. The
    cursor light can therefore never put light on the surface a word sits on,
    and the grain can never sit over a letterform.

    They are <i> rather than <b> deliberately: the shared ambient layer in
    styles/tokens.css drifts `[data-field] > b` and phases its children by
    :nth-child, so a third <b> here would join the drift and shift the phase
    offsets of the two real fields. A different tag joins neither.
  */}
  {/*
    AND THAT IS WHY `--2` IS AN <i> NOW. It is the same idiom, used for the same
    reason and for a second purpose: the tag is what decides whether a field
    breathes. One light in a section breathes and the fill light is still, which
    halves the always-on animation count without changing the composition by a
    pixel — a 1.00 to 1.04 scale on a 90px blur is not a thing anybody can see
    on its own, and A-111 counted fifteen of them.
  */}
  <div class="hero__wash" data-field="on" aria-hidden="true">
    <b class="hero__amb hero__amb--1"></b>
    <i class="hero__amb hero__amb--2"></i>
    <i class="sv-spot"></i>
    <i class="sv-grain"></i>
  </div>

  <div class="hero__inner">
    {/*
      The eyebrow capsule is the sitewide treatment, owner decision 2026-08-02,
      and it arrives from `.eyebrow` in styles/labels.css rather than being
      restated below. scripts/check-treatments.js rule 9 fails the build if the
      capsule is DECLARED anywhere but labels.css.

      WHAT IS LEFT HERE IS NOT THE RECIPE. This eyebrow introduces an h1 rather
      than the h2 every other eyebrow introduces, and the head block under it is
      taller, so it takes --stack-2 where the shared rule gives --stack-1. That
      is WHERE THE LABEL SITS rather than WHAT IT IS. `margin-block-end` alone,
      not a `margin: 0 0 X` shorthand, so the three zero sides keep coming from
      the shared rule and cannot silently diverge from it either.
    */}
    {/*
      `fx-shimmer` is the ONE instance of that recipe on the site, and the
      argument for putting it here rather than anywhere else is written in
      styles/effects.css above the rule. Short version: this line is the first
      thing on the page, it is never interactive, and nothing else ever happens
      to it, so a slow light costs nothing and competes with nothing.
    */}
    <p class="eyebrow hero__eyebrow fx-shimmer">Bid and tender software, supplier and buyer</p>

    {/*
      THE THIRD SENTENCE IS THE GRADIENT SENTENCE, and it is the third one on
      purpose: owner instruction, 2026-08-02. The post-award half is what this
      product is built around and what the site's whole narrative turns on, so
      it is the sentence that gets the light. That instruction is UNCHANGED by
      the 2026-08-07 rewrite recorded in this file's header: "Prove it." is the
      post-award half, named by what the product actually does to it. The first
      two sentences stay solid, because three gradient sentences would be a
      background, not an emphasis.

      THE FALLBACK IS THE POINT OF THE SPAN. background-clip: text needs a
      transparent fill, and an inherited -webkit-text-fill-color is exactly what
      caused this site's invisible-text P0. So the span carries a SOLID
      --c-teal in the unlayered base rule, and only inside the @supports gate
      does it become transparent with the gradient painted behind it. A browser
      without background-clip renders solid teal on --c-bg at 7.7:1, which is a
      deliberate emphasis rather than a degradation. Nothing outside this one
      span is touched, so there is no descendant for a text-fill to leak onto.
    */}
    <h1 class="hero__title" id="hero-title">
      Qualify. Draft. <span class="hero__prove">Prove it.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="hero__lede">Every figure traced to evidence you already hold.</p>

    {/*
      TWO REAL CONTROLS, NOT A BUTTON AND A TEXT LINK. Owner review: "'Learn
      more' is not in a button, why?" It is now the shared Button component's
      secondary variant, so the pair is one control system rather than a button
      beside a hand-rolled lookalike. The 54px height, the pill radius, the focus
      ring and the hover all come from ui/Button.astro and are not restated here.

      SECONDARY CHANGED FROM "LEARN MORE" TO THE FREE TOOL, R262-WEB-03,
      2026-08-08. Owner item, deferred at A-117 (2026-08-05) pending the
      deploy-source switch and now unblocked: the switch to astro/dist shipped
      2026-08-05 and is verified live. The homepage carried four competing
      calls to action: this pair plus FinalCta's own primary and free-tool link
      at the foot of the page. "Learn more" -> /crowmark was a third, different
      action competing with the two the page is meant to offer. It is replaced
      with the SAME free-tool link FinalCta already closes the page with
      (index.astro), so the whole homepage now only ever asks one of two
      things: request access, or read a tender free. The label needs no
      `label` override, unlike "Learn more" did, because "Read a tender, free"
      already names its own destination and FinalCta ships the identical text
      with no override either.

      THE PRIMARY IS UNTOUCHED. "Request access" also appears again in
      FinalCta at the foot of the page, and that is repetition of the same
      offer at two scroll depths, not a second competing action.
    */}
    <div class="btn-row">
      <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
      <Button href="/tools/tender-compliance-matrix/" variant="secondary">
        Read a tender, free
      </Button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/*
    THE CLOSING NOTE. Owner decision, and it is a restoration rather than a new
    line: it shipped on the old hero and was lost in the V6 redesign only
    because the approved frame had no element for it. It goes back.

    IT IS THE STRONGEST SENTENCE ON THE SITE AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. The
    competitive register this product is defined against sells autonomy. This
    says, in eleven words, that the engine does not clear its own gate and that
    a named human approves — which is a substantive claim about how the product
    works, in the same register as the section that watches the engine REFUSE a
    figure. Do not cut it for space and do not soften it into a benefit
    statement.

    IT STILL CLOSES THE HERO. It used to sit under the graphic; with the graphic
    gone it follows the button pair directly, which is the same position in the
    reading order — the reader meets the claim, the offer, and then its limit.

    NO NEW TYPE SIZE AND NO NEW COLOUR. --t-micro, --font-mono and the tokens
    the eyebrow already uses, so the hero still declares exactly two font sizes
    of small text.
  */}
  <p class="meta hero__gate">
    <span class="hero__gate-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    The engine cannot clear its own gate. A named person approves.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    /* Also the horizontal-overflow guard. The wash paints outside its box, and
       the sitewide gate fails a page whose scrollWidth exceeds its clientWidth
       at 320, 390, 768 or 1440. */
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: var(--sec-pad-y);
  }

  /* Sits behind everything the hero renders. z-index rather than a negative
     one, because a negative z-index would put it behind the page background on
     a stacking context this section does not control. */
  .hero__wash {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The scroll-driven parallax amplitude for this rig. Declared here rather
       than defaulted so the hero and the sections below it travel at visibly
       different speeds instead of moving as one sheet — see the @supports block
       in styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 48px;
  }

  /* ══ FOUR FIELDS, READ OFF THE DEPLOYED SITE ═══════════════════════════
   *
   * The owner compared screenshots three times and said the gradient was still
   * wrong. It was, and matching the page wash did not fix it because the wash is
   * not what dominates the hero. This rig is.
   *
   * Sampled at 25 fixed points on both pages at 1440x900, the mismatch was not
   * subtle and it was not a matter of degree:
   *
   *     60,120    live 31,19,62  (violet)   ours  8,15,23  (near black)
   *    360,300    live 26,17,54  (violet)   ours 18,57,59  (teal)
   *   1080,300    live 12,57,68  (cyan)     ours  9,13,21  (near black)
   *
   * LIVE PUTS VIOLET ON THE LEFT AND CYAN ON THE RIGHT. We had teal on the left
   * and violet low right, so the two halves of the hero were the wrong colours,
   * which is why no amount of tuning alpha ever closed it.
   *
   * These are the deployed geometries: four orbs, blur 90px rather than our
   * 160px, mix-blend-mode: screen, and SOLID colours rather than the
   * alpha-carrying --orb-* tokens, because screen blending over near-black is
   * what produces the light and a pre-multiplied alpha halves it.
   *
   *   1  violet  662px  top    -172  left -115   0.55   the source, high left
   *   2  cyan    605px  top      29  left  835   0.42   the answer, high right
   *   3  orchid  547px  bottom  -92  left  490   0.30   low centre
   *   4  teal    490px  bottom -169  left   86   0.34   low left, the falloff
   *
   * TWO OF THOSE FOUR SHIP, AND THE TABLE IS LEFT WHOLE BECAUSE IT IS THE
   * MEASUREMENT. `--1` keeps its hue and its geometry exactly. `--2` keeps its
   * geometry and loses its hue. `--3` and `--4` are gone. See the note in the
   * markup for the owner's rule and for why the cut lands here.
   *
   * ORBS 3 AND 4 HUNG FROM THE FLOOR, NOT THE CEILING, AND THAT IS WHY THEY
   * WENT FIRST. They were `top: 520px` and `top: 654px`, read off a hero that
   * measured 975px tall with the strata graphic in it; that hero is 508px now,
   * so both were cropped hard against the hero's bottom edge and both spilled
   * their light into the section beneath. A-103 measured the result at the
   * pixel — the hero band read [25,17,51] against the concept's [14,13,27]
   * while the ground below the hero already matched the concept exactly — so
   * these two were carrying the whole of the remaining tint.
   *
   * Sized in px rather than percentages so a field is the same physical size on
   * a phone and on a 32 inch monitor, which is the only way an ambient rig reads
   * consistently. */
  .hero__amb {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(90px);
    /* Screen, not normal. These are lights on a near-black page; at normal blend
       they are grey discs sitting on top of it. */
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .hero__amb--1 {
    width: 662px; height: 662px;
    top: -172px; left: -115px;
    opacity: 0.55;
    background: var(--orb-1);
  }

  /* THE ANSWER, HIGH RIGHT, AND IT IS LIGHT RATHER THAN COLOUR NOW.
     Geometry untouched from the table above; only the fill moved, from
     --orb-2 (cyan #22D3EE) to the ink role, which is white.

     --c-text rather than --c-white because a component names a ROLE and only
     tokens.css names a colour; scripts/check-palette-roles.js rule 1 fails the
     build on the latter, and the two are the same pigment under V6 anyway.
     The same substitution, for the same reason, is already in
     BothSides.astro's beams and in FinalCta's sweep.

     ── THE OPACITY IS 0.22 AND NOT THE 0.42 THE CYAN FIELD CARRIED, AND THAT
        IS A CORRECTION MADE AGAINST A RENDER RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.

     The first attempt matched LUMINANCE: screen-blending over near-black lands
     roughly `channel x alpha`, cyan carries about 0.69 of white's relative
     luminance, so 70% of white at the same 0.42 should have been the same
     brightness with the hue taken out. The arithmetic was right and the result
     was wrong. Measured at (1080,450) on the built page it went from
     [16,58,68] to [48,49,53] — the same luminance to within a unit, and it
     read as GREY FOG rather than as light, because a grey lift over near-black
     has no hue for the eye to attribute it to and is read as dirt on the
     screen instead.

     So an achromatic key has to be DIMMER than the chromatic one it replaces,
     not equal to it. Five candidate fills were rendered at 1440 and compared
     against the deployed hero: 70%/0.42 fogged, 45%/0.26 nearly vanished,
     violet at 0.26 put a second hue back, and deleting the field outright left
     the right half of the hero flat and the composition one-sided. 70%/0.22 is
     the one that lifts the high-right corner and stops. */
  .hero__amb--2 {
    width: 605px; height: 605px;
    top: 29px; left: 835px;
    opacity: 0.22;
    background: radial-gradient(
      circle,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 70%, transparent),
      transparent 66%
    );
  }

  /* The vignette, over the fields at normal blend because it darkens and a
     screen-blended dark layer does nothing. In the PAGE colour rather than
     black, and at absolute px rather than percentages, as above. */
  .hero__wash::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* ── OFF, AND MEASURED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * This darkened the hero back to the page colour towards its edges. With
     * the ambient rig built to live's geometry, the vignette is fighting the
     * design rather than supporting it: live carries no such layer over its
     * orbs, and its edge darkness comes from where the orbs are placed.
     *
     * A/B against the deployed page at 20 sampled points, vignette on vs off:
     *
     *     60,120   live 31,19,62    on 12,10,25  d37    off 37,23,68  d6
     *    360,120   live 35,21,69    on 25,16,51  d18    off 37,21,70  d2
     *   1080,120   live  8,26,34    on  8,18,26  d8     off 10,24,34  d2
     *
     * The top of the hero, which is the part a reader meets first and the part
     * the owner screenshotted, moves from badly under-lit to within a few
     * units. It costs a little at the far right edge (1380,120 goes from d3 to
     * d14), so this is a trade rather than a free win, and the mean across all
     * 20 points moves 21.4 to 20.8.
     *
     * Kept as `none` rather than deleted so the geometry above stays readable
     * and one line restores it. */
    --vig: 860px 900px at 50% 32%;
    background: none;
  }

  /* At phone width the two 700px fields are wider than the viewport and a
     160px blur flattens them into one violet-grey sheet, which loses the
     hero's edges rather than lighting them. Scaled with the hero; never
     switched off, because the glow is the design. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .hero__amb {
      filter: blur(96px);
      width: 380px;
      height: 320px;
    }
  }

  /* The shared measure, from tokens. Hardcoding 1160px and clamp(20px,5vw,48px)
     here is how the legacy page ended up with six different max-widths: each
     component knew the number, so no component could be corrected centrally. */
  .hero__inner,
  .hero__gate {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
  }

  /* Centred head block, owner decision 2026-08-02, and now the whole hero: see
     the header note for why removing the graphic does not make left alignment
     available. A blanket centring rule is exactly what produced the legacy
     defect of sixteen force-centred element types needing 138 per-element
     opt-outs across 24 pages, so this stops at `.hero__inner`. */
  .hero__inner {
    text-align: center;
  }

  .hero__eyebrow {
    margin-block-end: var(--stack-2);
  }

  .hero__title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h1);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h1);
    line-height: 1.04;
    /* ── THE TRACKING EASES AS THE TYPE GROWS, AND IT IS A RAMP NOT A STEP ──
     *
     * A-109. -0.02em is right at --t-h1's FLOOR and wrong at its CAP, because
     * tracking stated in em scales with the type: the same setting that reads
     * as tight at 2.6rem takes 1.664px out of every gap in the headline at
     * 5.2rem, INCLUDING the word space before "Prove it.", which is half of
     * why that phrase sat in the back of the sentence before it. The other half is the span's
     * own box and is dealt with further down.
     *
     * THE RAMP IS ONE ABSOLUTE VALUE HELD BETWEEN TWO em BOUNDS. 0.052rem is
     * 2% of --t-h1's floor (2.6rem x 0.02), and --t-h1's cap is exactly twice
     * its floor, so the SAME absolute value is 1% of the cap. The clamp
     * therefore resolves to exactly -0.02em at the floor, exactly -0.01em at
     * the cap, and eases continuously between the two at every size in
     * between. Nothing is flattened at the small end, where there was never a
     * complaint.
     *
     * A BREAKPOINT WAS THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE AND IS WORSE. Easing the
     * tracking at a `min-width` would change the headline's width by roughly
     * 0.01em x 23 characters at a single pixel of viewport — about 16px at
     * these sizes — inside an element that is `text-wrap: balance`d, so one
     * pixel of resize could flip the wrap. A ramp cannot.
     *
     * BOTH BOUNDS STAY IN em SO THE TYPE SCALE KEEPS OWNING THE SIZE. --t-h1
     * lives in styles/tokens.css and is not this component's to know. If its
     * floor or cap moves, this can still never track tighter than -0.02em nor
     * looser than -0.01em; the ramp constant simply lands elsewhere inside
     * that range, which is a safe drift rather than a defect. rem rather than
     * px because --t-h1 is itself expressed in rem, so the derivation survives
     * a user root font-size that is not 16px. */
    letter-spacing: clamp(-0.02em, -0.052rem, -0.01em);
    color: var(--c-text);
    /* One line at desktop, and at the widths where it cannot be, two even lines
       rather than four words and an orphan. Unsupported browsers get the normal
       greedy wrap, which is what they have today. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ── THE HEADING FADE, WHICH LIVE HAS AND WE DID NOT ────────────────────
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-03, comparing crowagent.ai against localhost:8095. They were
   * right and I had been checking the wrong thing: I compared the ONE phrase
   * they first mentioned and confirmed it matched, while every heading on the
   * page differed.
   *
   * Read from the deployed site, live paints h1, h2 AND h3 with
   * `linear-gradient(#FFFFFF 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0.6) 100%)` clipped to the
   * text. Ours were flat --c-text. That is a difference on every heading of
   * every page, which is why the page read as "the gradient is not there".
   *
   * --grad-heading ALREADY HELD EXACTLY THAT VALUE and was referenced by
   * nothing. The token was ported across from live and never wired up, so the
   * design system recorded the intent and the pages ignored it. A token nobody
   * references is not a decision, it is a note.
   *
   * IT IS APPLIED WHERE THE COLOUR IS SET, NOT GLOBALLY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
   * Astro's scoped component styles are unlayered, so a blanket `h1, h2, h3`
   * rule in a stylesheet would lose to every component that sets `color` on its
   * own heading. Applying it beside the colour it replaces is the only place it
   * reliably wins.
   *
   * THE FALLBACK IS THE SOLID COLOUR ABOVE, kept rather than removed, so a
   * browser without background-clip and forced-colours mode both get readable
   * text. `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent` INHERITS, which is exactly how
   * this codebase produced invisible text once before, so anything nested that
   * needs its own colour must set it. */
  @supports ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .hero__title {
      background-image: var(--grad-heading);
      /* ── THE RAMP TILES PER LINE, NOT PER BLOCK ────────────────────────
       *
       * A top-to-bottom fade painted over the whole element means a heading
       * that wraps renders its SECOND line entirely at the dim end. Seen on
       * /pricing: "See the price first." bright, "Sized for SMEs." grey. The
       * deployed site never shows it because its hero is one line.
       *
       * Tiling the background to one line-height gives every line the same
       * ramp, so the effect survives a wrap instead of turning into a fault.
       * `lh` is the correct unit and is recent, so an em fallback matching the
       * declared line-height goes first. */
      background-size: 100% 1.06em;
      background-repeat: repeat;
      -webkit-background-clip: text;
      background-clip: text;
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
      /* ── THE TAIL OF THE "y" WAS NOT BEING DRAWN ───────────────────────
       *
       * The background is clipped to the text but it is still only PAINTED
       * inside the element's box, and clipped text has no fallback colour — so
       * ink below the box is filled with nothing and is absent. Measured at
       * 1440 by comparing the lowest inked scanline with the treatment on
       * against the same glyphs in flat ink: SIXTEEN device pixels of the "y"
       * in "Qualify" existed and were not drawn. This heading is a single line
       * box at line-height 1.04, so its descender hangs 0.089em below the box
       * and there is no following line to catch it.
       *
       * Same construction and the same reasoning as styles/headings.css, which
       * carries the full note: 0.12em is about double the worst measured
       * overflow, and the margin subtracts it again so the box grows for PAINT
       * and the gap under the hero heading does not move.
       *
       * scripts/check-heading-ink.js fails the build if THE PADDING is removed:
       * its rule 2 measures the lowest inked scanline against the box, so a
       * clipped descender is caught. It does NOT measure the compensating
       * negative margin — there is no spacing assertion anywhere in that file —
       * so removing that half would pass every gate and silently open a gap under
       * the hero. Corrected 2026-08-04, A-57; this used to say "either half". */
      padding-block-end: 0.12em;
      margin-block-end: calc(var(--stack-2) - 0.12em);
    }
  }

  @supports (height: 1lh) {
    .hero__title { background-size: 100% 1lh; }
  }

  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .hero__title {
      background-image: none;
      color: CanvasText;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: CanvasText;
    }
  }

  /*
   * "GET PAID." IN THE HEADLINE, GRADIENT. Owner instruction, 2026-08-02.
   *
   * THE BASE RULE IS SOLID AND STAYS SOLID unless the gate below matches.
   * --c-teal on --c-bg measures 7.7:1, well over the 3:1 floor large text needs,
   * so a browser with no background-clip support renders a readable emphasised
   * sentence rather than a missing one. This ordering is not stylistic: this
   * site has already shipped a P0 in which an inherited -webkit-text-fill-color
   * made text invisible, and the only safe shape is "readable by default,
   * transparent only where the paint is guaranteed to arrive".
   *
   * SCOPED TO THIS SPAN AND NOTHING BELOW IT. The span has no element children,
   * so there is no descendant for a text-fill to leak onto, which is the exact
   * mechanism of the original defect.
   */
  .hero__prove {
    /* ── AN INLINE BOX CANNOT CARRY A CLIPPED GRADIENT SAFELY ──────────────
     *
     * A-109, second half, and this one is a PAINT fault rather than a spacing
     * one. This span had no display property anywhere in the file, so it was
     * `display: inline` while also carrying background-clip: text and a
     * transparent fill in the gate below. An inline box has no box on the
     * inline axis for a background to grow into — the paint area is the text
     * run — and the negative tracking above removes trailing advance from the
     * final glyph rather than adding it. So the right side bearing of the
     * closing "." fell outside the painted area, and with the fill
     * transparent, ink outside the paint is filled with NOTHING and is simply
     * absent. This is the same mechanism as the missing tail of the "y"
     * documented above the h1's padding-block-end, turned through 90 degrees.
     *
     * inline-block gives the span a real box, which is what makes the padding
     * below do anything at all; on an inline box it would be painted but never
     * widen the run the gradient is clipped to on the block axis.
     *
     * THE PAD CLEARS THE TRACKING DEFICIT AT BOTH ENDS OF THE SCALE, which is
     * the arithmetic that picks 0.04em rather than a feel. The eased tracking
     * takes 0.052rem — 0.832px at a 16px root — off the last glyph's advance
     * at EVERY size, because that ramp is one absolute value. 0.04em is
     * 1.664px at --t-h1's floor and 3.328px at its cap, so the painted box
     * clears the deficit by 2x at the small end and 4x at the large end, and
     * the remainder is the side bearing itself. It is trailing only: the
     * leading bearing of the "P" sits inside the word space before the span
     * and was never clipped.
     *
     * THE NEGATIVE MARGIN CANCELS THE PAD IN LAYOUT AND KEEPS IT IN PAINT.
     * Exactly the pair the h1 already uses twenty lines up: the box grows for
     * PAINT and the advance width does not move. The hero is owner-frozen, and
     * without this the extra 0.04em would sit at the end of a CENTRED line and
     * walk the headline's optical axis 0.02em to the left — 1.7px at the cap.
     * A change nobody asked for is still a change.
     *
     * THE BASELINE DOES NOT MOVE, AND THAT IS A CONDITION RATHER THAN A HOPE.
     * An inline-block takes the baseline of its last in-flow line box UNLESS
     * its `overflow` computes to anything other than `visible`, in which case
     * it takes its bottom margin edge and this phrase drops off the headline's
     * baseline by most of a line. Nothing sets overflow on this span. DO NOT
     * ADD ONE. Its internal line box uses the same inherited font and the same
     * inherited line-height as the run beside it, so the two baselines coincide
     * and the h1's line box height is unchanged.
     *
     * DECLARED HERE, IN THE UNLAYERED BASE, NOT IN THE @supports GATE BELOW.
     * The gate is allowed to change PAINT and nothing else. If display and
     * padding lived inside it, a browser without background-clip would lay this
     * headline out to a different width than one with it, and the two could
     * wrap differently — a layout fork on a capability query, which is the kind
     * of thing that is only ever found on the one browser nobody tested. The
     * 0.04em costs the solid-teal fallback nothing.
     *
     * IT CONSTRAINS THE WRAP AND THE CONSTRAINT IS THE ONE WE WANTED. An
     * inline-block is atomic, so "Prove it." can no longer break between its
     * two words; the only breaks left in the headline are after "Qualify." and
     * after "Draft.". At 390px the h1 sits at --t-h1's floor inside a 350px
     * content box (tokens.css drops --gutter to 20px below 420), and the phrase
     * measures comfortably under half that line, so it cannot orphan and it
     * cannot overflow. What is left is precisely what the `text-wrap: balance`
     * note on the h1 asks for: two even lines rather than four words and an
     * orphan. */
    display: inline-block;
    padding-inline-end: 0.04em;
    margin-inline-end: -0.04em;
    /* ── AND THE SAME PAIR ON THE BLOCK AXIS, FOR THE DESCENDER ─────────────
     *
     * OWNER REPORT 2026-08-05: "P bottom is still white". They were right, and
     * it is the same defect as the trailing bearing above, turned through 90
     * degrees, so it is fixed the same way.
     *
     * MEASURED ON THE BUILT PAGE at 1440, sampling the brightest ink per pixel
     * row through the "p" of "paid": rows up to y=305 read rgb(53,207,240),
     * the cyan of --grad-hero-text. Rows y=306 to y=311 read rgb(255,255,255),
     * PURE WHITE, and y=312 is the antialiased tail. The span's border box
     * bottom is 305.875 and the "p" glyph box runs to 314.34, so the descender
     * hangs 8.47px BELOW the box the gradient is painted into.
     *
     * WHY IT IS WHITE AND NOT SIMPLY MISSING, which is the part that identifies
     * the cause: the h1 ALSO carries background-clip:text with a transparent
     * fill (measured: its computed -webkit-text-fill-color is rgba(0,0,0,0)),
     * and its gradient is white. Where this span's background stops, the
     * PARENT's white background is still clipped to the same glyph and paints
     * the rest of it. So the tail is not unpainted, it is painted by the h1.
     *
     * 0.12em CLEARS 8.47px WITH MARGIN: at --t-h1's rendered 83.2px that is
     * 9.98px, and it scales with the type rather than being a fixed pixel that
     * only holds at one viewport. The negative margin cancels it in layout for
     * exactly the reason the inline pair does: the box grows for PAINT and the
     * line box does not move, so the owner-frozen hero keeps its geometry.
     *
     * STILL NO `overflow` ON THIS SPAN. The note above explains why: an
     * inline-block with a non-visible overflow takes its bottom margin edge as
     * its baseline instead of its last line box, and the phrase would drop off
     * the headline. Padding does not do that. DO NOT ADD ONE. */
    padding-block-end: 0.12em;
    margin-block-end: -0.12em;
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  @supports ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .hero__prove {
      /* ── THE SAME SPECTRUM AS THE SECTION CAPSULES ────────────────────────
       *
       * Owner, 2026-08-03: "make the hero Get paid. text gradient multicolour
       * like the section capsules have." The phrase was renamed to "Prove it."
       * on 2026-08-07 (see this file's header); the instruction is about the
       * GRADIENT SENTENCE, whichever sentence that is, so it still governs.
       *
       * CORRECTED an hour later, and the correction is the point. My first fix
       * used --grad-spectrum, the CAPSULE gradient. The live site does not paint
       * this phrase with the capsule gradient: read from the running legacy
       * build, the element that carried this gradient had three stops at
       * 100deg, teal through cyan to violet, with no pink. --grad-hero-text now
       * holds exactly that. See its note in tokens.css. */
      background-image: var(--grad-hero-text);
      -webkit-background-clip: text;
      background-clip: text;
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    }
  }

  /* Windows high-contrast throws user styling away, and a transparent fill
     there is an invisible sentence. Forced back to system text: an accessibility
     override, which tokens.css names as the one legitimate exception to the
     no-escalation rule. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .hero__prove {
      background-image: none;
      color: CanvasText;
      -webkit-text-fill-color: CanvasText;
    }
  }

  .hero__lede {
    margin: 0 auto var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-lede);
    line-height: 1.65;
    /* Matches .section__standfirst. Centred text needs a centred measure or the
       line sits left inside a centred block. Measured 813.6px at 1440, inside a
       1,064px content box, so this cap is what sets the line length and it did
       not move when the graphic was removed. */
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* NOTHING STYLES THE ACTIONS BUT `.btn-row`, which is `width: fit-content`
     with `margin-inline: auto` in styles/button-row.css and therefore centres
     itself rather than depending on the parent's text-align. */

  /* ── The closing note ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Centred with the head block above, sitting on the same measure so it lines
     up with the copy. --t-micro and --font-mono, borrowed from the eyebrow, so
     the hero introduces no new size. --c-text-sub measures 14.48:1 on --c-bg;
     it is quiet, not dim, and nothing here animates.

     NO LEADING OF ITS OWN, FROM 2026-08-04. It said 1.6, which made it one of
     three recipes `.meta` rendered on the built site — 20.8px here, 19.5px on
     /pricing, 16.25px on the other 175 instances. It carries `class="meta"`, so
     the leading is styles/labels.css's to state and it states it once. */
  .hero__gate {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    text-align: center;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* A single teal stop before the line. Teal is the verified colour and a
     named approver is the site's verification claim, so the mark is doing the
     same job it does everywhere else. It is decoration and aria-hidden: remove
     all colour and the sentence is unchanged, which is WCAG 1.4.1. */
  .hero__gate-mark {
    flex: none;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--c-verified);
    box-shadow: 0 0 8px var(--c-verified-glow);
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* At 350px of content box the dot and the sentence fight for the same line
       and the wrap lands badly. The mark goes; it carries nothing. */
    .hero__gate-mark { display: none; }
  }
</style>
